{
  "image_name": "0de0e78057e22f0ae67bc87768a24da2.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes the complement system as both an extracellular defense cascade and an intracellular regulator of cell behavior. On the left, three activation routes—classical, lectin, and alternative—converge on C3 convertases, which cleave C3 into C3a and C3b. This drives formation of C5 convertases, production of C5a and C5b, and ultimately the membrane attack complex. Multiple inhibitory proteins are shown at key steps, emphasizing tight regulation of complement activation.\n\nThe lower left highlights the traditional systemic role of complement in blood and extracellular spaces: clearing pathogens and harmful self-derived material.\n\nThe right side expands this view by showing complement activity in and around cells and tissues. Extracellularly generated C3a and C5a act through their receptors in autocrine and paracrine ways to promote effector functions such as cytokine production and proliferation. The bottom-right schematic depicts an intracellular “complosome,” where complement components and receptors appear to function inside immune and non-immune cells. These pathways connect to lysosomes, mitochondria, mTOR, RIG-I/MAVS signaling, metabolism, autophagy, and inflammasome activation, influencing nutrient use, respiration, reactive oxygen species, and inflammatory gene expression. Overall, the image conveys complement as a broad regulator of immunity, metabolism, and cell physiology, not just a serum killing system.",
